== Dependency order ==

<pre>
Semantics
  ↓
Ontology
  ├→ Entity
  ├→ Identity
  ├→ Concept
  ├→ Category
  ├→ Relation
  └→ Property
       ↓
Classification ↔ Taxonomy
       ↓
Knowledge representation
  ├→ Semantic network
  ├→ Knowledge graph
  └→ Schema / Metadata
       ↓
Semantic Web
  ├→ Resource Description Framework
  └→ Web Ontology Language
        ↓
  Description logic
        ↓
      Inference
</pre>

This is a writing dependency graph, not a claim that all semantics or ontology historically developed in this order.

== Normalized definitions ==

=== Semantics ===
Semantics concerns meaning and interpretation. In natural/formal languages it studies how expressions relate to meanings, referents, truth conditions or models. Formal semantics supplies mathematically explicit interpretations for a syntax.

=== Ontology ===
Ontology has two major senses:
# philosophical ontology: inquiry into what exists and the most general kinds/relations of what exists;
# information-science ontology: explicit, often formal specification of a conceptualization/domain vocabulary, including classes/categories, relations/properties and axioms/constraints.

=== Entity ===
An entity is anything treated as a distinct object of reference, quantification, description or representation within a discourse or model.

=== Identity ===
Identity is strict sameness: being one and the same thing. Identity criteria support re-identification across descriptions, times or representations.

=== Concept ===
A concept is a unit or constituent of thought/structured meaning used in categorization, reasoning and knowledge organization. Competing theories treat concepts as mental representations, abilities or abstract objects.

=== Category ===
A category is a grouping or kind used to distinguish entities, concepts or phenomena. In philosophy, categories can mean the most general kinds of beings or concepts.

=== Classification ===
Classification is the process/system of assigning items, instances, observations or concepts to categories/classes under stated criteria, rules or models.

=== Taxonomy ===
A taxonomy is an organized system of categories/concepts, commonly structured through broader–narrower or parent–child relations for classification/navigation.

=== Relation ===
A relation is a connection holding among two or more relata. Formal systems may model relations extensionally; ontologies and KR systems commonly use named relation types.

=== Property ===
A property is a characteristic, quality or formally represented predicate attributable to entities/classes. In RDF, a property is specifically a binary relation used as a predicate.

=== Knowledge representation ===
Knowledge representation is the study/practice of encoding knowledge into explicit structures that can serve as surrogates for a domain, communicate ontological commitments and support reasoning/computation.

=== Knowledge graph ===
A knowledge graph is a graph-based knowledge representation whose nodes/edges encode entities, concepts, types, relations, facts or other structured knowledge, often with schema, ontology, provenance, reasoning or learning components.

=== Semantic network ===
A semantic network is a graph representation in which nodes denote concepts/entities and labeled links denote semantic relations.

=== Schema ===
A schema is an explicit specification of expected or permitted structure of data/documents/messages/knowledge artifacts, such as fields, types, relationships and constraints.

=== Metadata ===
Metadata is structured information about resources used for description, discovery, management, preservation, interpretation, rights, provenance or use.

=== Semantic Web ===
The Semantic Web is the Web architecture/technology program for publishing and linking machine-interpretable data using global identifiers, shared vocabularies, formal semantics and interoperable standards.

=== Resource Description Framework ===
RDF is a W3C framework/data model for representing information as graphs of subject–predicate–object triples and datasets using IRIs, blank nodes and literals.

=== Web Ontology Language ===
OWL is a W3C ontology-language family with formally defined semantics for classes, individuals, object/data properties, class expressions and axioms, designed to support automated reasoning on Semantic Web data.

=== Description logic ===
Description logics are a family of logic-based knowledge-representation formalisms centered on concepts/classes, roles/relations and individuals, with well-defined reasoning tasks and controlled expressivity.

=== Inference ===
Inference is the process or relation of deriving/supporting conclusions from premises, evidence, rules, models or representations. Entailment is a semantic relation; an inference procedure is a process/algorithm that attempts to derive consequences.

== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
semantics ≠ syntax
semantics ≠ pragmatics
philosophical ontology ≠ information-science ontology
ontology ≠ taxonomy
ontology ≠ schema
ontology language ≠ ontology content
entity ≠ physical object
entity ≠ class/category
identifier ≠ identity
numerical identity ≠ qualitative similarity
concept ≠ word/label
concept ≠ class by definition
category ≠ taxonomy
classification ≠ taxonomy
classification scheme ≠ classifier algorithm
taxonomy parenthood ≠ necessarily subclasshood
relation ≠ property in every theory
property ≠ database field
knowledge representation ≠ represented world
knowledge graph ≠ arbitrary graph database
knowledge graph ≠ ontology
semantic network ≠ Semantic Web
schema ≠ ontology
schema validation ≠ inference
metadata ≠ only data about data
metadata vocabulary ≠ metadata instance
Semantic Web ≠ one database
RDF ≠ serialization syntax
RDF ≠ RDFS
RDF ≠ OWL
OWL ontology ≠ philosophical ontology
OWL class ≠ object-oriented class
owl:sameAs ≠ similarity
Description logic ≠ full first-order logic
entailment ≠ inference procedure
valid inference ≠ true premises
soundness ≠ completeness
</pre>

== Formal stack ==

<pre>
domain/reality
    ↓ conceptualization
ontology / taxonomy / schema / vocabulary
    ↓ formal representation
RDF / OWL / rules / other KR languages
    ↓ semantics
interpretations / models / entailment
    ↓ procedure
reasoner / classifier / theorem prover / query engine
    ↓ derived assertions / answers
</pre>

A system may use only some layers.

